<file path="vignettes/getting-started.qmd">
---
title: "Getting started with veryMAD"
description: >
  Annotate sequencing metadata with explicit MAD thresholds.
vignette: >
  %\VignetteIndexEntry{Getting started with veryMAD}
  %\VignetteEngine{quarto::html}
  %\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}
knitr:
  opts_chunk:
    collapse: true
    comment: "#>"
---

`veryMAD` estimates one median absolute deviation (MAD) threshold per selected
metric. Choose the metrics, tail directions, and transformations. Annotation
retains every input row; report mode returns flags and thresholds.

```{r}
#| label: setup
#| include: false
library(veryMAD)
```

## A basic QC workflow

This sequencing metadata example uses percentage points for `pct_mito`: 8
denotes 8%.

```{r}
sample_metadata <- data.frame(
  sample = paste0("sample_", 1:8),
  library_size = c(2.4e6, 2.5e6, 2.6e6, 2.7e6, 2.8e6, 2.6e6, 0.45e6, 2.5e6),
  detected_genes = c(12000, 12500, 13100, 12800, 13400, 12600, 1500, 12300),
  pct_mito = c(4, 5, 6, 5, 7, 6, 8, 29)
)

# Select the tail to review for each metric
qc_directions <- c(
  library_size = "lower",
  detected_genes = "lower",
  pct_mito = "upper"
)

# Add one flag per metric and a combined flag
qc_annotated <- mad_qc(
  sample_metadata,
  metrics = qc_directions,
  verbose = FALSE
)
```

The result keeps every row and adds logical flags:

```{r}
qc_annotated[, c("sample", "library_size_mad_outlier",
                 "detected_genes_mad_outlier", "pct_mito_mad_outlier",
                 "mad_qc_outlier")]
```

`mad_qc_outlier` is `TRUE` when any selected metric is flagged. Use the flags
to guide review in the analysis workflow.

## Inspect thresholds with a report

Use `output = "report"` to inspect thresholds and flags without adding columns
to the input.

```{r}
qc_report <- mad_qc(
  sample_metadata,
  metrics = qc_directions,
  output = "report",
  verbose = FALSE
)

qc_report$thresholds[, c("metric", "direction", "lower_raw",
                         "upper_raw", "status")]
qc_report$flags[, c("id", "mad_qc_outlier")]
```

The threshold table records each metric's status and observation counts.
</file>

<file path="vignettes/mad-scaling.qmd">
---
title: "Robust MAD scaling"
description: >
  Center and scale vectors and matrices with the median and MAD.
vignette: >
  %\VignetteIndexEntry{Robust MAD scaling}
  %\VignetteEngine{quarto::html}
  %\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}
knitr:
  opts_chunk:
    collapse: true
    comment: "#>"
---

`mad_scale()` centers numeric data by its median and divides by its median
absolute deviation (MAD). Set `center` or `scale` to `FALSE` to skip that
operation.

```{r}
#| label: setup
#| include: false
library(veryMAD)
```

## Scale a vector

This example scales expression summaries for five genes.

```{r}
gene_expression <- c(
  gene_A = 8.2,
  gene_B = 8.7,
  gene_C = 9.1,
  gene_D = 9.4,
  gene_E = 18.0
)

scaled_expression <- mad_scale(gene_expression)
round(scaled_expression, 2)
```

Names and missing values are preserved.

## Scale an expression matrix

For a matrix, `margin = 1` scales rows and `margin = 2` scales columns. Here
rows are genes and columns are samples.

```{r}
expression_matrix <- matrix(
  c(
    8.2, 8.5, 8.4, 8.6,
    9.1, 9.0, 9.3, 9.2,
    4.0, 4.2, NA, 4.1
  ),
  nrow = 3,
  byrow = TRUE,
  dimnames = list(
    c("gene_A", "gene_B", "gene_C"),
    c("sample_1", "sample_2", "sample_3", "sample_4")
  )
)

scaled_by_sample <- mad_scale(expression_matrix, margin = 2)
round(scaled_by_sample, 2)
```

Dimensions and dimnames are preserved. The default `zero_mad = "na"` returns
`NA` for zero-MAD groups. `"zero"` uses a unit denominator; centered results
are unscaled deviations. Choose `"error"` to stop on zero MAD.

```{r}
mostly_constant <- c(value_1 = 1, value_2 = 1, value_3 = 1, outlier = 100)
mad_scale(mostly_constant)
mad_scale(mostly_constant, zero_mad = "zero")
```
</file>

<file path="vignettes/qc-interpretation.qmd">
---
title: "Interpreting MAD QC flags"
description: >
  Read MAD flags and apply them in single-cell QC workflows.
vignette: >
  %\VignetteIndexEntry{Interpreting MAD QC flags}
  %\VignetteEngine{quarto::html}
  %\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}
knitr:
  opts_chunk:
    collapse: true
    comment: "#>"
---

MAD flags identify selected statistical tails for review. Choose a direction
for each metric:

- `lower` flags unusually small values;
- `upper` flags unusually large values;
- `both` flags either tail.

```{r}
#| label: setup
#| include: false
library(veryMAD)
```

## Read a flag in context

The example uses common sequencing metrics. `pct_mito` is in percentage
points: 8 denotes 8%.

```{r}
qc_metadata <- data.frame(
  sample = paste0("sample_", 1:7),
  library_size = c(2.4e6, 2.5e6, 2.6e6, 2.7e6, 2.8e6, 0.5e6, NA_real_),
  detected_genes = c(12000, 12500, 13100, 12800, 13400, 1600, 12200),
  pct_mito = c(4, 5, 6, 5, 7, 8, 30)
)

qc_result <- mad_qc(
  qc_metadata,
  metrics = c(
    library_size = "lower",
    detected_genes = "lower",
    pct_mito = "upper"
  ),
  output = "report",
  verbose = FALSE
)

qc_result$flags[, c("id", "library_size", "detected_genes",
                    "pct_mito", "mad_qc_outlier")]
```

The missing library size remains `NA` in its flag. The combined flag is `NA`
when no metric is flagged and at least one metric is unresolved. Review
individual flags with the source metadata.

## Threshold status and transformations

The threshold table reports calculation-scale and raw-scale limits.

```{r}
transformed_result <- mad_qc(
  qc_metadata,
  metrics = c(library_size = "lower", pct_mito = "upper"),
  transform = c(library_size = "log1p"),
  output = "report",
  verbose = FALSE
)

transformed_result$thresholds[, c(
  "metric", "direction", "transform", "lower_raw", "upper_raw", "status"
)]
```

`min_n` sets the minimum number of observations used for estimation. The
statuses `insufficient_n` and `all_missing` identify metrics without an
estimated threshold.

## Zero MAD and downstream decisions

A zero MAD means the data provide no non-zero robust spread estimate. Choose
the policy for the downstream analysis.

```{r}
constant_metric <- data.frame(
  sample = paste0("sample_", 1:6),
  detected_genes = rep(12000, 6)
)

zero_mad_result <- mad_qc(
  constant_metric,
  metrics = c(detected_genes = "lower"),
  zero_mad = "zero",
  output = "report",
  verbose = FALSE
)

zero_mad_result$thresholds[, c("metric", "mad", "lower_raw", "status")]
```

Use `zero_mad = "na"` to leave flags unresolved or `"error"` to stop. For
stratified analyses, split the data and call `mad_qc()` on each subset.

## Single-cell RNA-seq

Common RNA metrics are `nCount_RNA`, `nFeature_RNA`, and `percent.mt`. This
example checks lower count and feature tails, an upper mitochondrial tail, and
applies `log1p` to the count-like metrics. Seurat's `percent.mt` is usually on
a 0–100 scale: 8 means 8%.

```{r}
rna_metadata <- data.frame(
  sample = rep(c("sample_A", "sample_B"), each = 6),
  nCount_RNA = c(820, 910, 970, 1050, 1120, 100, 125, 150, 175, 190, 210, 230),
  nFeature_RNA = c(430, 480, 520, 570, 610, 55, 80, 95, 100, 115, 125, 140),
  percent.mt = c(5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 35, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9)
)

rna_metrics <- c(
  nCount_RNA = "lower",
  nFeature_RNA = "lower",
  percent.mt = "upper"
)
rna_transform <- c(nCount_RNA = "log1p", nFeature_RNA = "log1p")

rna_report <- mad_qc(
  rna_metadata,
  metrics = rna_metrics,
  transform = rna_transform,
  output = "report",
  verbose = FALSE
)
rna_report$thresholds[, c("metric", "direction", "transform", "status")]
```

## Single-cell ATAC-seq

ATAC metadata may include `nCount_peaks`, `TSS.enrichment`, and
`nucleosome_signal`. The example selects lower peak-count and TSS tails and an
upper nucleosome-signal tail. Names and useful directions depend on the
pipeline and assay; these are not universal thresholds.

```{r}
atac_metadata <- data.frame(
  nCount_peaks = c(1200, 1800, 3200, 6000, 9000, 20000, 2500, 4100),
  TSS.enrichment = c(0.8, 1.3, 3.0, 5.4, 8.0, 15.0, 4.1, 6.2),
  nucleosome_signal = c(3.5, 2.4, 1.8, 1.4, 1.1, 0.9, 1.6, 1.3)
)

atac_report <- mad_qc(
  atac_metadata,
  metrics = c(
    nCount_peaks = "lower",
    TSS.enrichment = "lower",
    nucleosome_signal = "upper"
  ),
  transform = c(nCount_peaks = "log1p"),
  output = "report",
  verbose = FALSE
)
atac_report$thresholds[, c("metric", "direction", "transform", "status")]
```

## Per-sample QC and absolute criteria

Estimate thresholds per sample by splitting metadata and calling `mad_qc()`
on each subset. Sample-specific thresholds can hide a uniformly low-quality
sample; also review between-sample distributions.

```{r}
sample_reports <- lapply(
  split(rna_metadata, rna_metadata$sample),
  function(x) mad_qc(
    x,
    metrics = rna_metrics,
    transform = rna_transform,
    output = "report",
    verbose = FALSE
  )
)
sample_reports$sample_A$thresholds[, c("metric", "lower_raw", "status")]
```

MAD flags identify outliers in the supplied reference distribution. Combine
them with separately chosen absolute criteria when appropriate. These example
cutoffs are illustrative, not general recommendations:

```{r}
rna_annotated <- mad_qc(
  rna_metadata,
  metrics = rna_metrics,
  transform = rna_transform,
  verbose = FALSE
)

illustrative_keep <- !rna_annotated$mad_qc_outlier &
  rna_annotated$nCount_RNA >= 500 &
  rna_annotated$percent.mt <= 20
table(illustrative_keep, useNA = "ifany")
```

## Seurat metadata

`mad_qc()` adds flags to Seurat cell metadata. The example checks cell order
against the original identities.

```{r}
if (requireNamespace("SeuratObject", quietly = TRUE)) {
  counts <- matrix(
    seq_len(40) %% 6,
    nrow = 5,
    dimnames = list(paste0("gene_", 1:5), paste0("cell_", 1:8))
  )
  seurat_object <- SeuratObject::CreateSeuratObject(counts = counts)
  seurat_object[["percent.mt"]] <- c(4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 30)
  cell_ids <- colnames(seurat_object)

  seurat_metadata <- seurat_object[[]]
  expected_flags <- mad_qc(
    seurat_metadata,
    metrics = rna_metrics,
    transform = rna_transform,
    verbose = FALSE
  )
  seurat_annotated <- mad_qc(
    seurat_object,
    metrics = rna_metrics,
    transform = rna_transform,
    verbose = FALSE
  )

  stopifnot(identical(rownames(seurat_annotated[[]]), cell_ids))
  stopifnot(identical(
    seurat_annotated[[]]$mad_qc_outlier,
    expected_flags$mad_qc_outlier
  ))
  head(seurat_annotated[[]][, c("nCount_RNA", "nFeature_RNA",
                                "percent.mt", "mad_qc_outlier")])
}
```
</file>

